@page "/help/colregs"

@*
    Quick-reference page for COLREGS Rules 13-15 (overtaking / head-on /
    crossing). Linked from the AIS popup's COLREGS row and reachable
    from the nav menu so the helm can drill in when the CPA banner
    surfaces a classification they want to verify before committing
    to a manoeuvre.

    Intentionally short: this is helm-side first-aid, not a full IRPCS
    course. The official reference link at the bottom takes the helm
    to the IMO source for anything beyond the three crossing rules.
*@

<PageTitle>Help: COLREGS</PageTitle>

<article class="content px-4 py-3" style="max-width: 880px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1 class="mb-3">COLREGS — quick reference</h1>

    <p class="text-muted">
        COLREGS (the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, also called "the Rules of the Road")
        describe who turns when two vessels approach each other. OnaPlotter classifies the encounter for power-driven
        vessels under Rules 13-15 and surfaces the role on the AIS popup + the CPA alarm banner. This page explains
        what the categories mean and what action they imply.
    </p>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h5">Rule 13 — Overtaking</h2>
        <p>
            A vessel approaching another from a position more than 22.5° abaft her beam (i.e. coming up from
            behind through the rear cone) is the <em>overtaking</em> vessel and must keep clear. The vessel
            being overtaken is the stand-on vessel and holds course and speed.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-muted">
            <li>OnaPlotter shows: <strong>"Overtaking"</strong> (you are catching up, give way) /
                <strong>"Being overtaken"</strong> (target catching up, stand on).</li>
            <li>Rule 13 overrides Rules 14 and 15 - once an overtaking situation exists it stays one until
                the overtaking vessel is finally past and clear.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h5">Rule 14 — Head-on</h2>
        <p>
            Two power-driven vessels meeting on reciprocal or nearly reciprocal courses (within ±22.5° of dead
            ahead) are head-on. Both alter course to starboard so each passes the other on the port side.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-muted">
            <li>OnaPlotter shows: <strong>"Head-on"</strong>. There is no give-way / stand-on distinction -
                both vessels turn.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h5">Rule 15 — Crossing</h2>
        <p>
            Two power-driven vessels on crossing courses where there is risk of collision. The vessel that has
            the other on her starboard side gives way and shall avoid crossing ahead.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-muted">
            <li>OnaPlotter shows: <strong>"Crossing (P)"</strong> - target is on YOUR port side, you are
                the stand-on vessel.</li>
            <li>OnaPlotter shows: <strong>"Crossing (S)"</strong> - target is on YOUR starboard side, you
                are the give-way vessel.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h5">Roles</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Give way</strong> - alter course early and substantially. The classic move on a
                stbd-side crossing is a starboard turn that takes you behind the target's stern.</li>
            <li><strong>Stand on</strong> - maintain course and speed. Rule 17 lets the stand-on vessel
                manoeuvre to avoid collision once it becomes apparent the give-way vessel isn't taking
                action; sound at least five short blasts when in doubt.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h5">Important caveats</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>OnaPlotter classifies <strong>power-driven vessel encounters</strong> only. Rule 12 (sailing
                vessels meeting) and Rule 18 (priority order between sail / power / restricted-in-ability /
                fishing / not-under-command / RAM) are not implemented.</li>
            <li>The classification is a hint. The helm always retains responsibility for collision avoidance
                under Rule 2 (responsibility) and the requirements of good seamanship - if the rule's action
                doesn't fit the situation, deviate from it.</li>
            <li>"Indeterminate" appears when one or both vessels are stationary or moving slower than ~0.2 kn,
                or when the encounter geometry doesn't resolve into a rule (parallel courses, etc.). No
                give-way / stand-on label is shown in that case.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 class="h5">Source</h2>
        <p>
            Full text and amendments: the IMO publishes the Convention on the International Regulations for
            Preventing Collisions at Sea (COLREG) at
            <a href="https://www.imo.org/en/OurWork/Safety/Pages/Preventing-Collisions.aspx"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                imo.org / Preventing Collisions
            </a>.
            Many jurisdictions republish the rules verbatim with national amendments; check what applies in
            your waters.
        </p>
    </section>
</article>
